theory.intro:
When all **three sides** are known, the cosine rule rearranges to find any angle. Put the side **opposite the angle you want** as $c$; the other two sides are $a$ and $b$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (V) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4.2,0);
\coordinate (A) at (1.3,2.7);
\draw (V)--(B) node[midway,below] {$a$};
\draw (V)--(A) node[midway,above left] {$b$};
\draw (B)--(A) node[midway,above right] {$c$};
\pic[draw,"$\theta$",angle radius=0.7cm,angle eccentricity=1.7] {angle=B--V--A};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\coordinate (V) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (4.5,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (3.141,2.674);
\draw (V)--(P) node[midway,below] {$12$};
\draw (V)--(Q) node[midway,above left] {$11$};
\draw (P)--(Q) node[midway,above right] {$8$};
\pic[draw,"$\theta$",angle radius=0.5cm,angle eccentricity=1.4] {angle=P--V--Q};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the angle $\theta$, correct to $1$ decimal place.

foundation.solution_text:
$\cos\theta=\dfrac{a^{2}+b^{2}-c^{2}}{2ab}$
$\cos\theta=\dfrac{11^{2}+12^{2}-8^{2}}{2(11)(12)}$
$\theta=\cos^{-1}\left(\dfrac{11^{2}+12^{2}-8^{2}}{2(11)(12)}\right)$
$=40.41\ldots^{\circ}$
$\approx 40.4^{\circ}$
